/-
# One radial nef model works throughout a short open interval
Let `g : Y ⟶ S` be a projective morphism of projective complex varieties, `Y` a `ℚ`-factorial
threefold, `(Y, B)` and `(Y, C)` real klt pairs, `D = K_Y + B` nef over `S`, and
`D_t = K_Y + (1 - t) B + t C`. For `0 < t < ε` the boundary `(1 - t) B + t C` is again real klt:
on a `ℚ`-factorial variety every `ℝ`-divisor is `ℝ`-Cartier, and the log discrepancy of a divisor
over `Y` moves affinely along the segment from `B` to `C`. So if `D_t` is not nef over `S`, the
real klt cone and contraction theorem gives a `D_t`-negative extremal ray `R` of `NE̅(Y/S)` and its
contraction `f`, with `ρ(Y/Z) = 1`; hence `f` contracts some curve `A`, whose class lies in `R`
and is `D_t`-negative, so `D_t` is negative on all of `R ∖ 0`. Small radial perturbations have
only `D`-null negative rays, so `D · A = 0`, and then `D_{t₀} · A = (t₀ / t) (D_t · A) < 0`,
against the nefness of `D_{t₀}`.
On a fixed contracted curve the degree of `D_t` is affine in `t` and nonnegative on the whole
interval, so it vanishes at one interior point only if it vanishes identically: otherwise it would
be negative just beyond that point, on the side away from a point where it is positive.
## Main results
* `ThreefoldProgramPort.RadialNefInterval.main_result`.
## References
* [J. Kollár, S. Mori, *Birational geometry of algebraic varieties*][KollarMori], §3.7
-/
